    h1: "How Are Boise Foothills Hillside Homes Engineered?",
    answer: "Building on a Boise foothills lot means building on a slope of clay-rich soils where gravity, water, and geology all work against a flat-lot foundation. Homes there are engineered from the ground up for the hillside, not adapted from flat-land details. Direct answer: the engineer starts with a geotechnical report that sets pier or stepped-footing foundations into stable material, designs engineered retaining walls for cut slopes, balances cut and fill in the grading plan, steps the floor framing to follow the slope, and builds drainage systems that keep water off and out of the hillside  -  permitted through the City of Boise's hillside review or Ada County for unincorporated sites, with the AHJ deciding approvals.\n\nThe foothills' geology sets the terms. The slopes are mantled with colluvium and clay-rich soils that creep slowly downhill, lose strength when saturated, and can slide where cuts are too steep or water is allowed to concentrate. Winter rain and spring snowmelt soak the upper soils exactly when the ground is least able to drain, and irrigation from uphill landscaping can feed a slope for months. The geotechnical engineer maps the soil profile, tests shear strength, evaluates global slope stability, and tells the design team how steep a cut can stand, where the stable bearing layer lies, and what drainage the slope needs. Nothing structural is drawn before that report exists.\n\nFoundations on steep lots typically bear on drilled piers or on stepped footings excavated into competent material, with stem walls that step down the slope in level increments. Where the house bridges across the slope, grade beams span between piers and the floor system is framed to step with the terrain rather than fighting it. Retaining walls  -  whether concrete cantilever, segmental block, or soldier pile  -  are engineered structures with their own geotechnical parameters, drainage, and global stability checks; a tall wall that holds back a saturated slope carries real loads and gets a real design. Downhill creep is the quiet enemy: the engineer details foundations and walls so slow soil movement does not accumulate into cracked walls and tilted floors over the years.\n\nDrainage is the discipline that holds the whole hillside design together. Surface water is intercepted above the house with berms or swales and carried around the structure in lined channels; subsurface drains relieve water pressure behind retaining walls and under slabs; and roof water goes to controlled discharge points, never onto the slope face below. Because a failed drain can saturate a slope that was stable when dry, the engineer designs drainage with redundancy and maintainable outlets. The hillside review  -  the City of Boise's foothills standards for annexed land, Ada County's hillside provisions beyond city limits  -  checks grading, walls, drainage, and wildfire interface together, and review timelines run longer than flat-lot permits because the submittal carries more engineering.",
