        heading: "Moisture: Adobe's Main Enemy",
        body: "Water dissolves adobe  -  slowly, grain by grain, until a wall base that looked solid crumbles under a probe. The attack comes from below as rising damp wicking up through the foundation, from the side as rain splash rebounding off hard ground, and from above as roof leaks and failed parapet caps. The engineer reads the moisture story in the wall: softened lower courses, salt efflorescence marking the evaporation line, and plaster that has debonded in sheets. The cure is to break every water path  -  regrade so water flows away, extend roof overhangs or add drip edges, repair the roof, and in persistent cases install a French drain to intercept groundwater.\n\nMaterial compatibility decides whether the repair lasts. Lime plaster and mud plaster breathe: they absorb moisture and release it, flex microscopically with the wall, and can be patched indefinitely. Portland cement stucco does none of this  -  it is rigid, nearly impermeable, and stronger than the adobe behind it, so trapped moisture softens the brick while the stucco shell hides the damage until courses collapse inside the wall. Restoration specifications therefore prohibit cement plaster on historic adobe and require lime-based finishes, a requirement the landmarks reviewers enforce in the historic districts. The wall must be allowed to dry; every detail serves that rule.",
