    description: "Defensible space around Reno homes is engineered as graded fuel-break zones  -  a noncombustible perimeter at the house, thinned vegetation beyond it, and ember-resistant detailing  -  coordinated with local fire district requirements.",
    h1: "How Is Wildfire Defensible Space Engineered Around Reno Homes?",
    answer: "Around Reno, where high-desert grasses and wind-driven fires meet hillside neighborhoods, the ground between a home and the wildland is engineered as carefully as the structure itself. Defensible space is not landscaping taste  -  it is a designed fuel system with the single purpose of denying a wildfire the fuel it needs to reach and ignite the house. Direct answer: the engineer lays out defensible space in zones  -  roughly five feet of noncombustible material at the structure, managed low-fuel landscaping to about thirty feet, and thinned native vegetation to about one hundred feet on steeper slopes  -  with graded fuel breaks, driveways sized for fire apparatus, and a reliable water supply, all designed to the local fire district's standards and reviewed by the AHJ.\n\nEmbers drive the design. Most homes lost in wildfires ignite not from direct flame contact but from wind-blown embers landing on roofs, in gutters, under decks, and against siding  -  sometimes a mile or more ahead of the fire front. That fact shapes every zone: the innermost zone exists to give embers nothing to ignite within the few feet where radiant heat from the house's own ignition would be unsurvivable anyway, and the outer zones exist to break up the fuel bed so the fire drops from a running crown fire to a ground fire that firefighters can actually engage. Cheatgrass, the invasive annual grass carpeting much of the Reno foothills, cures early and burns fast, which is why the outer thinning has to be maintained rather than done once.\n\nThe civil engineer turns those zones into construction documents. The site plan shows the fuel-modification zones with their boundaries dimensioned from the structure, the grading that creates level fuel breaks and removes ladder fuels on slopes, and the driveway profile  -  width, grade, and turnarounds sized for fire apparatus, because a home the engines cannot reach cannot be defended. Water supply is engineered alongside: hydrant spacing and flow where municipal water exists, or on-site storage with fire-department connections where it does not. On sloped lots the zones extend farther downhill, since fire runs uphill faster and preheats the fuels above it.\n\nDefensible space is a maintenance covenant, not a construction milestone. Grasses regrow, shrubs resprout, and a single neglected season can restore the fuel bed the grading removed  -  which is why fire districts inspect, why HOAs write maintenance into their CC&Rs, and why the engineer specifies maintainable plant palettes rather than just clearing everything. In the Reno area the applicable requirements depend on jurisdiction: the City of Reno, Washoe County, and the Truckee Meadows Fire Protection District each play a role depending on where the parcel sits, and their standards, inspection programs, and review timelines differ. The AHJ decides what is approved, and no defensible space design promises a home will survive  -  it is engineered to give the structure and its firefighters a fighting chance.",
    directAnswer: "Defensible space around Reno homes is engineered in zones: about five feet of noncombustible material at the structure, managed low-fuel landscaping to roughly thirty feet, and thinned native vegetation to about one hundred feet on slopes  -  with graded fuel breaks, fire-apparatus access, and reliable water supply, all to the local fire district's standards.",
