    h1: "How Are Denver-Area WUI Homes Hardened Against Wildfire?",
    answer: "Where the Denver metro meets the foothills  -  the wildland-urban interface, or WUI  -  homes face wind-driven wildfires that throw embers far ahead of the flame front. Hardening is the engineering of the building envelope to deny those embers and that heat a way in. Direct answer: Denver-area WUI homes are hardened with Class A fire-rated roof coverings, noncombustible or ignition-resistant siding, ember-resistant attic and foundation vents, tempered dual-pane windows, enclosed eaves and soffits, and noncombustible decks, fences, and mulch near the structure  -  designed to the county or municipal wildfire code that applies to the site and reviewed by the local AHJ, which decides approvals.\n\nThe foothills setting defines the threat. Jefferson, Douglas, and Boulder counties' foothill communities sit in ponderosa and scrub oak fuels on slopes that accelerate fire spread, and frontal winds can carry embers across entire subdivisions. The Marshall Fire showed the metro's own vulnerability: grass-fueled, wind-driven fire moving through developed areas. The engineering response does not try to make a house fireproof  -  no wood-frame home is  -  but to eliminate the small vulnerabilities where most structure ignitions begin: the vent, the gutter, the deck board, the mulch bed.\n\nThe envelope is hardened from the top down. The roof gets a Class A covering  -  the highest fire rating  -  over solid sheathing, with valleys and edges detailed so embers cannot lodge. Attic, eave, and foundation vents get corrosion-resistant fine mesh that blocks embers while preserving airflow, or listed ember-resistant vent devices. Eaves and soffits are enclosed with noncombustible materials so heat cannot enter the attic through open framing. Walls get noncombustible or ignition-resistant siding with the bottom courses protected from ground-level ember accumulation. Windows become tempered dual-pane units that resist the thermal shock that shatters single-pane glass and opens the interior to fire. Decks, fences, and mulch within the first several feet of the house go noncombustible, because each is a fuse leading fire to the wall.\n\nThe code side is county-driven in much of the WUI. Colorado's foothill counties have adopted wildfire regulations  -  some as amendments to the building code, some as standalone WUI codes  -  that specify exactly which construction provisions apply in mapped hazard areas, and the requirements step up with the hazard level. Inside municipalities the city's code governs, which may or may not include WUI provisions depending on the city's adoption. That patchwork is why the engineer verifies the parcel's jurisdiction and the applicable wildfire provisions before specifying a single material: the same house on opposite sides of a county line can face different hardening requirements, different review checklists, and different inspection hold points.",
