        body: "An adobe wall a foot or more thick does not behave like insulated frame construction. Heat moves through it slowly - the technical term is thermal lag - so the afternoon heat reaches the interior in the evening, and the night's coolness arrives by morning. In Santa Fe's climate, with large day-night swings and abundant sun, that lag is free climate control. The retrofit's first job is to protect it: no exterior insulation that isolates the mass from the interior, no dark impermeable coatings that trap moisture, no demolition of mass the building needs.\n\nWhere insulation is genuinely needed - typically the roof and the interior face of walls in the coldest exposures - the team uses vapor-open assemblies. Interior framed walls with mineral wool or similar insulation let the adobe dry inward and outward while the new layer slows heat flow. Lime-based insulating plasters can add modest R-value without changing the wall's appearance or breathability. Each assembly is checked for condensation risk at the adobe interface, because a retrofit that wets the wall trades energy savings for structural damage.",
