        body: "Stormwater design near a canal is defined by a prohibition: no site drainage into the irrigation channel. The civil engineer sizes detention or retention to the city or county stormwater manual, routes all outfalls to an approved discharge point, and grades the site so everyday flows and major storms both stay out of the canal. Water quality treatment is provided per the manual before discharge. The design also protects the canal during construction - silt fence, stabilized entrances, and phased grading keep sediment out of the water while the site is open.\n\nCrossings are engineered structures with district oversight. A driveway or utility crossing a canal needs the hydraulic opening the district requires, structural design for the loads it will carry, and details that protect the canal lining and banks during and after construction. The submittal to the district includes the hydraulic calculations, the structural design, and the construction access plan - the district reviews it on its own timeline, which the project schedule must accommodate. Landscaping near the canal is selected and placed to preserve maintenance access and avoid root damage to the banks.",
