    slug: "intermountain-west-geothermal-heat-pump-design",
    title: "How Are Geothermal Heat Pumps Designed for the Intermountain West?",
    description: "Geothermal heat pump design for the Intermountain West sizes the ground loop - vertical boreholes or horizontal trenches - to the building's loads and the region's soils, then matches equipment to design temperatures.",
    h1: "How Are Geothermal Heat Pumps Designed for the Intermountain West?",
    answer: "The Intermountain West gives geothermal heat pumps a lot to work with: cold winters that punish air-source equipment, dry soils in some areas and workable geology in others, and owners motivated by long-term operating cost. But a ground-source system is only as good as its loop design - undersize the borefield and the system limps through January. Direct answer: the engineer calculates the building's heating and cooling loads, characterizes the soil and groundwater with test borings or thermal conductivity testing, sizes the ground loop - vertical boreholes, horizontal trenches, or a hybrid - to meet the dominant load across the region's cold winters, and selects heat pump equipment rated for the entering water temperatures the loop will actually deliver.\n\nLoad calculation comes first and it must be honest about winter. Heating dominates in most of the region - the loop is typically sized for the heating load with cooling as the check - and the engineer uses the local design temperatures, not a national default. Internal gains, ventilation, and the envelope are all in the calculation, because an accurate load is the difference between a borefield that works and one that slowly freezes its own ground over successive winters. The engineer also decides the system's role: full heating and cooling, or a hybrid where the geothermal handles the base load and a boiler or furnace covers the peaks - a legitimate cost saver on large commercial projects.\n\nThe ground loop is the engineered heart. Vertical boreholes - typically a few hundred feet deep - suit tight sites and deliver stable temperatures, with the bore count, depth, and spacing set by the thermal conductivity testing and the building's annual heating and cooling imbalance. Horizontal trenches or slinky loops cost less to install where land is available but need the acreage and interact with seasonal surface temperatures. Groundwater changes everything: moving groundwater carries heat to the loop and can dramatically improve performance, while dry, low-conductivity soils do the opposite - which is why the test boring matters more than any rule of thumb. The loop design includes the grout specification, the headering, antifreeze selection for freeze protection, and the pumping energy, because a loop that costs too much to pump is a failed design.\n\nEquipment selection and controls close the loop, literally. Heat pumps are selected for capacity and efficiency at the entering water temperatures the ground loop will produce in the coldest week - catalog ratings at mild conditions do not count. Distribution - forced air, radiant floors, or water-to-water for snowmelt - is designed for the lower supply temperatures heat pumps prefer, which often means radiant or oversized air coils rather than the high-temperature baseboards a boiler would feed. Permitting varies by jurisdiction: some cities and counties require well permits for vertical boreholes through the health or water authority in addition to the building mechanical permit, with different forms and timelines. The engineer confirms the AHJs - plural - before drilling. The authorities decide approvals; the design package gives each one what it needs.",
    directAnswer: "Geothermal heat pumps for the Intermountain West are designed by calculating the building's heating and cooling loads, testing soil and groundwater conditions, sizing a ground loop - vertical boreholes or horizontal trenches - to match those loads across the region's cold winters, and selecting heat pump equipment rated for the design temperatures.",
