        heading: "Loop Configurations and Equipment Matching",
        body: "Vertical boreholes are the default for most Intermountain West projects: they fit small lots, reach stable year-round ground temperatures, and scale by adding bores. The design sets the number, depth, and spacing from the thermal analysis, details the U-bend piping and thermally enhanced grout, and lays out the headering trench back to the building. Horizontal trenches trade drilling cost for land - cheaper per foot of loop where acreage exists, but exposed to seasonal temperature swings and competing with everything else buried on the site. Open-loop systems using groundwater directly are possible where water rights and water quality allow, but they carry regulatory and maintenance burdens the engineer prices honestly.\n\nEquipment is matched to the loop, not the catalog headline. The engineer selects water-to-air or water-to-water heat pumps by their capacity and efficiency at the minimum entering water temperature the loop will deliver - the coldest-week condition - and verifies part-load performance for the shoulder seasons that dominate run hours. Distribution is designed for heat-pump-friendly temperatures: radiant floors, low-temperature air coils, or dedicated snowmelt zones, each with the pumping and controls to match. The controls sequence the heat pumps, any supplemental heat, and the loop circulators so the system runs efficiently across the whole year, and commissioning verifies flows, temperatures, and capacities against the design.",
